findings.body:
**Treat a failed `ps` probe as a live pid in `runLiveness`.**

`procCmdline` does not handle `ps` failures. When the probed process exits, `ps` returns a nonzero status, and promisified `execFile` rejects ([Node.js `child_process`](https://nodejs.org/api/child_process.html)). `runLiveness` awaits `procCmdline` at `src/adapters/process/liveness.ts:184-185` without a catch. The rejection therefore ends the liveness check before `runState` returns a state.

The impact depends on the cause. A process that exits between the `pidAlive` check and the `ps` call fails one pass. The next pass recovers because `pidAlive` is then false and `ps` is not called. A `ps` failure that persists while a detached review is alive rejects every pass. `collectFinished` then exits before its remaining items and `reconcileCompleted`, and `runSlot` exits before `saveQueue`. Admissions stop until `ps` works or the live run ends.

Keep a pid alive when its `ps` probe fails. `pidAlive` has already reported it alive, so the failed probe cannot prove it gone. Do not make `procCmdline` return `''` instead. An empty command line can let `runState` return `harvest` while the CLI pid still runs.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```diff
+  // A failed `ps` probe cannot prove the pid exited, so the pid stays alive.
+  const commandLineMatches = async (pid: number, needle: string): Promise<boolean> => {
+    try {
+      return (await procCmdline(pid)).includes(needle);
+    } catch {
+      return true;
+    }
+  };
   const runnerAlive =
-    pid !== null && pidAlive(pid) && runId !== null && (await procCmdline(pid)).includes(runId);
+    pid !== null && pidAlive(pid) && runId !== null && (await commandLineMatches(pid, runId));
-  const cliAlive = cli !== null && pidAlive(cli) && (await procCmdline(cli)).includes('coderabbit');
+  const cliAlive = cli !== null && pidAlive(cli) && (await commandLineMatches(cli, 'coderabbit'));
```

</details>
